{"name":"Baroque Grey","hex":"#5f5d64","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"baroque","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Ornate, intricate, decorated, laden with detail.","Complex and beautiful, despite an outward irregularity."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An ornate, detailed style."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/baroque"},{"term":"grey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray.","Synonym of coloured (pertaining to the mixed race of black and white)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey"}],"entries":[{"title":"Baroque","blocks":[{"text":"The Baroque is a Western style of architecture, music, dance, painting, sculpture, poetry, and other arts that flourished from the early 1600s until the 1750s. It followed Renaissance art and Mannerism and preceded the Rococo and Neoclassical styles. It was encouraged by the Catholic Church as a means to counter the simplicity and austerity of Protestant architecture, art, and music, though Lutheran Baroque art developed in parts of Europe as well."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/WLA_metmuseum_Venus_and_Adonis_by_Peter_Paul_Rubens.jpg/330px-WLA_metmuseum_Venus_and_Adonis_by_Peter_Paul_Rubens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baroque"},{"title":"Grey","blocks":[{"text":"Grey or gray is an intermediate color between black and white. It is a neutral or achromatic color, meaning that it has no chroma. It is the color of a rain or storm cloud, of ash, and of lead."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg/330px-Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey"}],"i